  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nThe gusty northerly winds will decrease in magnitude after 7 pm tonight.\nA light northerly wind is expected through the overnight hours and\nthen again tomorrow. The northerly winds will advect drier in across\nthe FA over the weekend with lower dewpoints. This will be good news,\nas humidity levels will be lower Saturday and Sunday. Highs will\nbe close to near normal values with highs peaking in the lower to\nmid 90s. Lows both mornings will be mainly in the 60s Saturday evening\nand then a mix of 60s to 70s Sunday morning. No storms are expected\nboth days.\n\nA 500 hPa anticyclone will move from the SW US to over the greater\nregion through next week. This will support the notion of a mainly\ndry forecast and hot highs. Highs will trend warmer each day. Upper\n90s to low 100s are likely for much of the next business week. Lows\nwill be warm with more 70s rather than 60s as each morning progresses.\nThe NBM has very low slight pops out west Tuesday evening. A look\nat the global models and their ensemble counterparts do not support\nthese storms chances much. Much of the ensembles are dry for next\nweek. With such a strong anticyclone over the FA, it would make more\nsense to not see any storms than to see them.\n\nLooking beyond next week, the ensembles continue to paint the picture\nof a prevailing mid level ridge across the Great Plains. This would\nsuggest for a dry and hot forecast with temperatures trending to\nbe above normal as we head into the middle portion of August.",
